.he "unique (1)                   9/30/26                   unique (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
PROGRAM
.br
.in +5
.bd
unique
- eliminate adjacent duplicate lines in an
.nf
         I/O stream
.fi
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
USAGE
.in +5
.br
.bd
unique [ -n ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.br
.in +5
.bd
Unique
reads the standard input and writes the standard output.
All but one of each set of adjacent duplicate lines are eliminated.
Otherwise, the text is copied through unchanged.
.sp
If the
.bd
-n
option is given, a count of the number of duplicates of each line is
printed along with the text of the line.  The count is the number of
adjacent copies, the line itself included, and it is separated from the
text by a tab.
.sp
The first argument and only the first is looked at, and it counts only
when it is
.bd
-n
exactly.  Unique takes no file names; the stream is fed to it by the
shell.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.br
.in +5
.nf
To eliminate all duplicate lines in a file:
.sp
.in +8
.bd 2
sort <file >temp
unique <temp >output
.in -8
.sp 2
.in -5
.fi
.bd
NOTES
.br
.in +5
In order to assure adjacency, the file in question must be presorted.
.sp
The program is v7's uniq, and this is the interface micronix gave it.
The page above is the 1982 one, with the two things added that the port
and the 1982 binary both do and the page does not say: the count and the
line are separated by a tab, and the option is read from the first
argument only.
.sp
V7 has more of it than micronix kept.  Its -u keeps only the lines that
never repeat and its -d only the ones that do; a +n skips n characters
before comparing, and a -n with a number after it skips n fields.  None
of those are here, and the 1982 binary has none of them either - it
swallows them and copies the stream, which is what this does.  V7 also
takes an input file and an output file and reopens them onto the
standard streams; this takes neither, and the shell does the redirection
the example shows.
.sp
A line of a thousand characters or more is refused with the message
"line too long" and the exit status is 1.  The buffers are v7's
thousand bytes and v7 had no bound on them; the check is this port's.
A line that runs off the end of a buffer writes over whatever the
compiler put next, which is not the same in any two builds, and the
1982 binary - same buffers, no bound either - mangles such a line
instead, and mangles it differently for that reason.
.sp
The last line of a file that does not end in a newline is lost, which
is v7's gline and the 1982 binary's behavior as well.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
